---
title: Japanese Chicken Rice in Rice Cooker
servings: 4
course: dinner
cuisine: Japanese
tags: quick, one-pan, make-ahead, chicken, rice
image: https://www.marmiton.org/assets/images/default-recipe-picture_80x80-6PRRwF3.jpg
source: https://www.marmiton.org/recettes/recette_riz-poulet-japonais-au-rice-cooker_224166.aspx
prep time: 10m
cook time: 45m
---

-- the source says "4 riz rond japonais", which is 4 rice-cooker cups, about 600 g — not 400 g.
-- At 400 g the 710 ml of liquid here makes porridge. It also asks for 200 g of chicken, which is
-- 50 g a serving; 500 g makes this four real meals rather than rice with a garnish.

-- everything goes in cold and the machine does the rest. The only work is the chopping.
-- the source's 50 ml of soy and 20 g of sugar are written here as spoons, which is what the
-- pantry is measured in and what you actually reach for at 9pm.

Wash the @sushi rice{600%g} until the water runs clear. Soaking it for ~{15%minutes} gives a better grain, but skip it if you want to eat sooner — chop while it sits.

Cut the @chicken thigh{500%g} into bite-sized pieces, the @carrot{1} into matchsticks and the @mushroom{100%g} into slices.

-- boneless thigh fillet is $24.99/kg at Royal Oak and bone-in thighs are $7.99. 700 g of bone-in
-- laid on top whole costs $7 less than 500 g of fillet, needs no cutting at all, and the meat
-- pulls off with two forks once it is cooked. Worth it on a night like this one.

-- the source uses 50 ml of soy across four servings. That is 2.3 g of salt a plate, and two
-- plates is most of a day's 5 g, so this is 2.5 tbsp instead: 1.7 g, and the mirin still carries it.

Put the @&sushi rice{}, @soy sauce{2.5%tbsp}, @mirin{4%tbsp}, @sugar{1.5%tbsp}, @water{600%ml}, @&mushroom{} and @&carrot{} into the #rice cooker{}. Stir once so the sugar dissolves and the rice sits flat.

Lay the @&chicken thigh{} on top without stirring it in — it steams above the rice and its juices run down through it.

Start the #rice cooker{} on the normal setting and leave it, about ~{45%minutes} for this much rice. Do not open the lid.

Fluff it together so the chicken and vegetables run through the rice, and scatter over @?spring onion{2}(sliced) and @?sesame seed{1%tsp}.

-- three of these four portions get eaten over the next day or two. Spread it out to cool rather
-- than leaving the pot warm, fridge it within the hour, and reheat covered in the #microwave{}
-- with a splash of water. Rice left sitting warm is the one leftover worth being careful with.
